<BaseLayout title="AMI Science" description="The wellness science and engineering ideas behind adaptive metabolic navigation." lang="en">
  <section class="section hero compact-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Science</p><h1>Science matters when it clarifies a decision.</h1><p class="lede">AMI uses metabolic science to support clearer decisions through honest engineering language — trajectory, not promised outcomes.</p></section>

  <section class="section stack"><article class="card highlight"><p class="eyebrow">1</p><h2>Why metabolic direction matters.</h2><p>Metabolic signals relate to energy, hunger, recovery, body composition, and long-term resilience. AMI shows trajectory; it does not promise results.</p></article><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">2</p><h2>Fasting and autophagy.</h2><p>Fasting windows can become useful behavioral metrics. AMI presents autophagy as an engineering model for adaptation and metabolic direction — estimates, not clinical measurements.</p></article><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">3</p><h2>Glucose and insulin context.</h2><p>Glucose and insulin patterns can provide context for why the same habit affects different people differently.</p></article><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">4</p><h2>Liver-related context.</h2><p>Liver-related indicators can matter because metabolism is a dynamic system with direction, context, and trajectory.</p></article><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">5</p><h2>Biomarkers.</h2><p>Labs can add context when they are interpreted as directional signals over time and reviewed appropriately.</p></article><article class="card blue-card"><p class="eyebrow">6</p><h2>Long-term metabolic habits and scientific foundations.</h2><p>AMI keeps scientific language useful: enough to support credibility, precision, and clearer decisions — without overclaiming.</p></article></section>

  <section class="section card highlight"><p class="eyebrow">Next step</p><h2>Science becomes useful when it clarifies a decision.</h2><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/ami/experience">See AMI in action</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
